WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.649 --> 00:00:08.649
OK, everyone.

00:00:08.649 --> 00:00:09.649
Good morning.

00:00:09.649 --> 00:00:14.860
This is Jack Vanhorn from the BD2K Training
Coordinating Center based at the University

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:15.860
of Southern California.

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:21.840
And I'd like to welcome everyone to the next
in our series of big data to knowledge, guide

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:24.150
to the fundamentals of data science.

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:29.070
Today, we're going to be hearing from Dr.
Zachary Ives from the University of Pennsylvania.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:33.940
He's going to be discussing data provenance
challenges, benefits, and research.

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:40.159
Dr. Ives is a professor of computer and information
science at the University of Pennsylvania

00:00:40.159 --> 00:00:44.400
as well as the newlyappointed Associate Dean
for Masters and Professional Programs at the

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:47.659
University of Penn's School of Engineering
and Applied Science.

00:00:47.659 --> 00:00:52.360
He's the co-founder of Blackfin, Inc., which
is a company focused on enabling life sciences

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:55.320
research and discovery through data integration.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:01:00.830
And his research interests include data integration,
sharing, management of big data, sensor networks,

00:01:00.830 --> 00:01:04.290
data provenance, and authoritativeness.

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:08.650
He's the recipient of an NSF Career Award
and an alumnus of the DARPA Computer Science

00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:12.690
Study Panel and Information Science and Technology
Advisory Panels.

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:16.400
He's co-authored textbooks, many influential
articles.

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:21.080
And we're absolutely delighted to hear about
his work on data provenance.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:28.040
That is the attaching of metadata, but also
by whom that data comes from, under what conditions,

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:32.890
the processing steps that were used, and all
the things that we call provenance.

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:35.490
So Zachary, thank you very much for joining
us.

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:37.440
We really look forward to your lecture.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:38.440
Great.

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:41.270
Thanks very much, Jack, and thanks, everyone,
for joining.

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:50.110
As Jack said, I have worked on a variety of
topics broadly related to computational support

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:53.700
for bringing together data from many places.

00:01:53.700 --> 00:02:00.880
And of course, once you start to integrate
and reuse data, all sorts of questions arise

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:06.090
about where the original data came from, and
how it was assembled, and so on.

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:14.980
And so I see provenance as a key part not
only of data science, but especially of data

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:17.859
science when you're trying to share and reuse
data.

00:02:17.859 --> 00:02:24.920
So my goal today is to try to give a high-level
overview of the main abstractions and models

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:29.840
for how we think about provenance, some of
the benefits of provenance, some of the open

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:35.410
challenges, and a little bit of a hint at
where a lot of the open problems in the active

00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:37.359
research is in this space.

00:02:37.359 --> 00:02:44.700
I should probably note, as I go into this,
that the NIH BD2K program is currently funding

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:48.439
three different efforts in the provenance
space.

00:02:48.439 --> 00:02:54.749
So there's our group at Penn, there's a group
out of Case Western, and a group out of Duke,

00:02:54.749 --> 00:03:00.169
and we're all trying to work together in a
coordinated fashion to make services that

00:03:00.169 --> 00:03:06.090
might be reusable across the broader BD2K
portfolio.

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:13.279
So with that, let me see if I can actually
dive in a little bit into why we actually

00:03:13.279 --> 00:03:17.250
care about provenance.

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:20.860
As I do that, I should probably emphasize
there are actually several different terms

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:23.250
used for roughly the same ideas.

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:26.799
So I think
the standard at this point is data provenance.

00:03:26.799 --> 00:03:32.810
And in fact, to my best knowledge, the term
was actually coined by my former colleague,

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:35.669
Peter Buneman, here at Penn.

00:03:35.669 --> 00:03:40.090
But other people have called it data lineage,
data pedigree, and so on.

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:46.889
And in some sense, really, it's an expanded
version of the scientific record, and the

00:03:46.889 --> 00:03:51.790
way we describe our experimental methodology,
and so on.

00:03:51.790 --> 00:03:56.430
So as you think about a process like what's
visualized on the slide where we go from data

00:03:56.430 --> 00:04:02.339
acquisition to data analysis to generating
figures and reports and papers, all along

00:04:02.339 --> 00:04:09.480
the way, we'd like to record in as much detail
as we can-the processes, the assumptions,

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:18.459
the sample size, the sample settings, et cetera
that went into the creation of each of the

00:04:18.459 --> 00:04:22.660
data products along the way.

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:26.250
When you look at that, at the very earliest
stage, the left-most, the data acquisition

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:31.810
side, what we tend to think of as provenance
is things like who did the experiment, under

00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:37.340
what instrument settings, and what the context
was.

00:04:37.340 --> 00:04:43.430
This is largely metadata that we need to carefully
define.

00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:48.871
As we move from acquisition of the raw data
from the real world, there typically are a

00:04:48.871 --> 00:04:51.740
lot of computational steps performed.

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:55.350
You run a piece of code with an algorithm.

00:04:55.350 --> 00:04:59.919
So there, we tend to think about data provenance
as something that might, for instance, be

00:04:59.919 --> 00:05:08.949
automatically created, maintained, and logged
by the computer system itself as you're running

00:05:08.949 --> 00:05:10.979
your code and so on.

00:05:10.979 --> 00:05:14.919
So there's a fairly different way we think
about provenance at the first stage versus

00:05:14.919 --> 00:05:18.500
the second stage, because the first stage
is primarily figuring out what you want to

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:19.500
capture.

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:23.530
The second stage is something that you can
imagine computationally supporting and automating.

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:28.860
And I'll get into more detail about that as
we go forward.

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:38.159
With respect to why provenance, obviously,
one of the big motivations for data provenance

00:05:38.159 --> 00:05:43.350
is support for reproducibility and validation
of results.

00:05:43.350 --> 00:05:49.080
The more documentation we have on exactly
what was done, the more detail we can understand

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:51.699
how to repeat the same results.

00:05:51.699 --> 00:05:57.750
But in fact, increasingly, we're starting
to put value on other uses of provenance.

00:05:57.750 --> 00:06:03.541
So one of the most important that I've looked
at in my computer science research is if you

00:06:03.541 --> 00:06:11.919
have data from many different providers, ultimately,
we can use data provenance and past history

00:06:11.919 --> 00:06:20.000
and reliability and so on to actually use
provenance to reason about how much you as

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:25.060
an individual user might want to trust or
rank or look at a particular data source.

00:06:25.060 --> 00:06:32.220
So really, the reliability, relevance, or
value of data may also depend very much on

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:35.310
its provenance.

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:39.699
So we tend to think of that generically as
trust, but it really encompasses lots of different

00:06:39.699 --> 00:06:40.870
things.

00:06:40.870 --> 00:06:46.860
Another key place where provenance comes into
play all the time is if I am bringing together

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:54.550
data that was acquired under different conditions,
as I'm trying to regularize it and harmonize

00:06:54.550 --> 00:06:59.620
it in order to conduct some kind of analysis,
I may have to process data from different

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:01.400
sources in different ways.

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:03.819
And that processing might involve more than
just converting

00:07:03.819 --> 00:07:07.069
the formats, which we typically do in the
data wrangling stage.

00:07:07.069 --> 00:07:14.969
It might also involve regularizing it in some
particular way, particularly paying attention

00:07:14.969 --> 00:07:23.150
to things like which populations were studied
in the different sources, and so on.

00:07:23.150 --> 00:07:33.039
So data provenance helps us not just reproduce,
but also evaluate and select and assign trustworthiness

00:07:33.039 --> 00:07:34.430
to data.

00:07:34.430 --> 00:07:39.560
And finally, if we look at data provenance
and we have a bird's eye perspective of what

00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:45.419
data sources are used where, then in fact,
much like we do with social networks and the

00:07:45.419 --> 00:07:51.789
worldwide web and so on, we can use, for instance,
information about how often a data source

00:07:51.789 --> 00:07:59.810
is used to give some indications about how
authoritative or useful or relevant that particular

00:07:59.810 --> 00:08:00.990
data source would be.

00:08:00.990 --> 00:08:09.310
So you can think of something analogous to
the H index to Google Page Rank or whatever

00:08:09.310 --> 00:08:13.919
being applied to data sources if we have visibility
through data provenance.

00:08:13.919 --> 00:08:16.919
So lots of different reasons to use it.

00:08:16.919 --> 00:08:24.279
I want to stress that data provenance and
data are really going to be tightly linked

00:08:24.279 --> 00:08:26.539
in our world view all the time.

00:08:26.539 --> 00:08:32.719
Even though we tend to think of data and metadata
as separate things, obviously, this is a kind

00:08:32.719 --> 00:08:38.970
of meta data that's extremely useful in understanding
how to interpret a given piece of data.

00:08:38.970 --> 00:08:43.720
So that's the basic overview of what I want
to talk about today.

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:51.320
Now what I'm going to do is drill down a little
bit to more formally explain what provenance

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:52.320
needs to be.

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:57.960
So we'll spend a bit of time on that, and
then I'll go through how we actually model

00:08:57.960 --> 00:09:01.840
it computationally with tools, and then some
of the applications.

00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:06.980
So starting with the formal definition, let's
separate into two camps.

00:09:06.980 --> 00:09:13.210
As I said, one of the kinds of data provenance
is provenance over data as we're acquiring

00:09:13.210 --> 00:09:14.210
it.

00:09:14.210 --> 00:09:19.650
So for instance, in the figure here, we have
an MRI machine.

00:09:19.650 --> 00:09:28.900
We might need to annotate the individual images
or the set of images with information about

00:09:28.900 --> 00:09:35.760
what the instrument settings were, who did
the actual experiment or data acquisition,

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:40.700
potentially who the subject was, what experiment
was being done, and so on.

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:46.140
And if you think of the classic questions
of who, what, where, when, why, and how, basically,

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:51.950
what we're trying to do is capture all of
those-- the environment, the context, the

00:09:51.950 --> 00:09:54.450
questions, et cetera.

00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:58.500
All of this is ultimately fairly domain-specific.

00:09:58.500 --> 00:10:05.310
It will depend on what the experiments are
about.

00:10:05.310 --> 00:10:12.450
But it does involve some piece of capturing
data from the instrument itself, and it may

00:10:12.450 --> 00:10:20.110
involve some additional data entry from the
people actually doing the data acquisition.

00:10:20.110 --> 00:10:25.851
So one of the big challenges here is, at some
level, data provenance, if you think of it

00:10:25.851 --> 00:10:30.700
as an explanation of exactly why you were
doing some particular data acquisition, can

00:10:30.700 --> 00:10:35.690
be an extremely, extremely detailed record
of what you've done before and a justification

00:10:35.690 --> 00:10:38.850
for what you're doing now.

00:10:38.850 --> 00:10:44.740
We never want to make provenance capture too
much of a burden, so the question is just

00:10:44.740 --> 00:10:48.210
how much of this context needs to be captured.

00:10:48.210 --> 00:10:55.020
And I think here, when we think about data
provenance, the key challenge is trying to

00:10:55.020 --> 00:11:00.030
decide exactly what kind of metadata we're
going to capture that balances between how

00:11:00.030 --> 00:11:05.490
hard it is and how much manual labor is involved
versus the level of detail captured that might

00:11:05.490 --> 00:11:10.980
help somebody else in the future reinterpret
and reuse our data.

00:11:10.980 --> 00:11:18.480
So this first stage is really primarily a
metadata design problem as opposed to a computational

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:20.890
tools problem.

00:11:20.890 --> 00:11:26.310
We have to think about things like how much
of the context of the data acquisition can

00:11:26.310 --> 00:11:30.860
we get, how much is actually going to be useful
in the future.

00:11:30.860 --> 00:11:37.460
Certainly, one of the big questions, especially
with modern sensors and data acquisition devices,

00:11:37.460 --> 00:11:42.610
is if we can capture a lot of that context
automatically where a human doesn't have to

00:11:42.610 --> 00:11:45.890
do anything, that's extraordinarily helpful.

00:11:45.890 --> 00:11:50.950
And there are lots and lots of examples of
this happening in practice.

00:11:50.950 --> 00:11:55.970
My collaborators, who do high-throughput gene
sequencing, there's a lot of parameter information

00:11:55.970 --> 00:11:58.250
that comes out of that machine.

00:11:58.250 --> 00:12:02.360
Whenever you take a digital photo on your
smartphone, there's the so-called EXIF data

00:12:02.360 --> 00:12:07.770
that includes all sorts of information about
the settings and potentially even GPS and

00:12:07.770 --> 00:12:08.990
so on.

00:12:08.990 --> 00:12:14.460
Increasingly, people are looking at using
NFC or barcodes or various other things to

00:12:14.460 --> 00:12:20.070
automatically capture their setup in the real
world so that nobody has to manually type

00:12:20.070 --> 00:12:24.370
in, or even worse, hand-write in the metadata.

00:12:24.370 --> 00:12:33.800
So in general, lots of questions here with
no standardized across the world, across domain

00:12:33.800 --> 00:12:40.110
guidelines, but rather, some general rules
of thumb on what you are trying to capture.

00:12:40.110 --> 00:12:47.670
However, there are also lots of standards
available at this point for what to capture

00:12:47.670 --> 00:12:51.970
in what fields, and how to name them, and
what units with respect to this initial data

00:12:51.970 --> 00:12:52.970
acquisition.

00:12:52.970 --> 00:12:59.140
And so for instance, one of the standards
bodies that our group interacts with quite

00:12:59.140 --> 00:13:01.180
often is Open mHealth.

00:13:01.180 --> 00:13:07.220
They have a set of predefined fields for a
lot of kinds of data acquisition tasks that

00:13:07.220 --> 00:13:11.870
they've tried to standardize across the community.

00:13:11.870 --> 00:13:18.550
Generally here, this is a standards effort
that focuses on particular domains.

00:13:18.550 --> 00:13:21.600
Technology can help, for instance, by automating
capture.

00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:30.110
But much like database design, the key tasks
here are human-intensive and involve capturing

00:13:30.110 --> 00:13:33.110
the experimental setup.

00:13:33.110 --> 00:13:39.870
Where the more computational and software
support can come in is when we switch from

00:13:39.870 --> 00:13:44.940
that initial data acquisition to later processing
of the data.

00:13:44.940 --> 00:13:53.480
So what I have here in the slide is a very
simple sketch of some kind of gene sequence

00:13:53.480 --> 00:14:01.160
capture and alignment job where we might start
off on the left with gene sequence information

00:14:01.160 --> 00:14:07.430
from initial data acquisition which was captured
with some additional metadata going through

00:14:07.430 --> 00:14:10.630
these two red boxes that represent computational
steps.

00:14:10.630 --> 00:14:16.190
For instance, in a Python script, the first
one might filter out low-quality sequence

00:14:16.190 --> 00:14:17.190
information.

00:14:17.190 --> 00:14:21.880
The second one might be actually trying to
align the gene sequences against some reference

00:14:21.880 --> 00:14:24.680
genome producing some set of alignments.

00:14:24.680 --> 00:14:28.110
So as we look at this, there are multiple
computational steps.

00:14:28.110 --> 00:14:34.380
There was provenance in terms of metadata
attached to the original sequence data.

00:14:34.380 --> 00:14:39.380
Now the question is what do we attach to the
output of the quality filter, what do we attach

00:14:39.380 --> 00:14:47.000
to the output of the aligner, and ideally,
can we build tools that help us do that automatically.

00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:51.530
And then ultimately, once we've captured it,
can we build tools that help us to reason

00:14:51.530 --> 00:14:56.280
about and make use of this kind of provenance
metadata.

00:14:56.280 --> 00:15:02.330
So conceptually, what we want to do is take
those computational steps and essentially

00:15:02.330 --> 00:15:06.660
create a trace or a log of here are the individual
steps.

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:09.790
Each step involved certain inputs.

00:15:09.790 --> 00:15:12.970
It had certain parameters and produced a certain
output.

00:15:12.970 --> 00:15:20.060
So schematically, on the right, you see a
list of steps, like read a particular file,

00:15:20.060 --> 00:15:24.870
do some computation like sorting rows, do
some computation, for instance, by computing

00:15:24.870 --> 00:15:28.530
a median, do some computation like writing
to a file.

00:15:28.530 --> 00:15:36.330
That is a kind of an example of what we might
want to capture to document our computational

00:15:36.330 --> 00:15:37.570
processes.

00:15:37.570 --> 00:15:41.040
And then, of course, we want to attach it
to our output.

00:15:41.040 --> 00:15:46.730
So from a tooling perspective, what we want
is, for instance, to be able to run a script

00:15:46.730 --> 00:15:55.470
that automates the production of output results
from input files, and we want to potentially

00:15:55.470 --> 00:15:59.290
store that output, either in the file system
or in a database.

00:15:59.290 --> 00:16:03.850
And effectively, on the side, what we'd like
is something equivalent to a provenance database

00:16:03.850 --> 00:16:12.090
system whose content will also link closely
to the file, as we schematically see in the

00:16:12.090 --> 00:16:13.980
right-hand of the figure.

00:16:13.980 --> 00:16:18.810
Again, we need to be able to reason about
provenance and data together, because we will

00:16:18.810 --> 00:16:27.110
often want to assess the data or interpret
the data based on its linked provenance annotations.

00:16:27.110 --> 00:16:30.080
So conceptually, it's a simple problem.

00:16:30.080 --> 00:16:35.420
Of course, when you try to actually break
it down into solvable steps, it ends up getting

00:16:35.420 --> 00:16:37.420
a little more complicated.

00:16:37.420 --> 00:16:42.340
So let's start with a simple, obvious first
take at it.

00:16:42.340 --> 00:16:48.260
I'll describe why we need to slightly tweak
that and then where that ultimately has taken

00:16:48.260 --> 00:16:50.160
the data provenance community.

00:16:50.160 --> 00:16:59.220
So the simplest form of capturing data provenance
is, again, during our initial data acquisition,

00:16:59.220 --> 00:17:04.539
as we read data from an instrument, we'll
attach to the file specific metadata tags

00:17:04.539 --> 00:17:13.300
and values, for instance, about the experiment,
and the configuration, and date of acquisition,

00:17:13.300 --> 00:17:19.410
and the subject, and whatever else is relevant
to our particular experiment.

00:17:19.410 --> 00:17:28.140
Then, as we support computational processing,
we could build some kind of service that essentially

00:17:28.140 --> 00:17:35.120
captures all of the events that the computer
is handling as it, for instance, launches

00:17:35.120 --> 00:17:42.170
a program, opens a file, writes to another
file, reads from another file, starts another

00:17:42.170 --> 00:17:43.970
script, and so on and so forth.

00:17:43.970 --> 00:17:51.070
So we can monitor program execution, capture
the program-specific events or tasks, feed

00:17:51.070 --> 00:17:56.410
it to some logging service, and then put it
into an event log, which we might think of

00:17:56.410 --> 00:17:59.290
as a primitive provenance database.

00:17:59.290 --> 00:18:05.630
And in fact, in the computer science world,
there are a lot of different research efforts

00:18:05.630 --> 00:18:10.060
that have been undertaken
using exactly this model.

00:18:10.060 --> 00:18:15.680
Probably the most well known was Margo's Seltzer's
work at Harvard on a system called Pass, which

00:18:15.680 --> 00:18:25.280
basically updated the operating system to
record all of the individual steps in reading

00:18:25.280 --> 00:18:26.740
and writing to files.

00:18:26.740 --> 00:18:32.200
And in fact, more recently, my own group has
built a system we call Provenance Tracker,

00:18:32.200 --> 00:18:36.240
which I'll talk about in more detail later,
which does something similar without changing

00:18:36.240 --> 00:18:37.570
the operating system.

00:18:37.570 --> 00:18:44.950
It essentially captures all of the data processing
steps in terms of jobs being run and inputs

00:18:44.950 --> 00:18:52.350
and outputs, and then it makes it accessible
for the future.

00:18:52.350 --> 00:18:54.480
So there are lots of tools for doing this.

00:18:54.480 --> 00:19:01.180
The nice thing about these tools is they capture
all the individual steps, the inputs and outputs,

00:19:01.180 --> 00:19:02.180
automatically.

00:19:02.180 --> 00:19:07.720
We don't have to worry about a human entering
all of that information.

00:19:07.720 --> 00:19:12.680
There also is a very related thing that happens
at pretty much every internet company and

00:19:12.680 --> 00:19:19.790
any large enterprise, which is very close
to provenance, but people tend to not think

00:19:19.790 --> 00:19:27.780
of it that way, which is as they process data,
they record logs and logs and logs of requests,

00:19:27.780 --> 00:19:34.880
for instance, from the web server, of individual
queries handled, of individual steps performed.

00:19:34.880 --> 00:19:40.120
All of that stuff is really capturing a kind
of data provenance, but it's being done in

00:19:40.120 --> 00:19:41.980
a fairly ad hoc way.

00:19:41.980 --> 00:19:48.320
And in general, for all of these log-based
approaches, if I just take the output of whatever

00:19:48.320 --> 00:19:54.660
the computer actually captures, it's generally
too low to be tremendously useful for these

00:19:54.660 --> 00:20:01.790
questions of reproducibility and these questions
of trustworthiness and reliability and impact.

00:20:01.790 --> 00:20:09.220
And the reason is it captures all the micro-level
steps that a piece of software read 64 bytes

00:20:09.220 --> 00:20:17.020
from one file and wrote 32 bytes to another
file and all sorts of information about sequential

00:20:17.020 --> 00:20:19.480
steps that may not matter.

00:20:19.480 --> 00:20:23.760
It's basically very, very low level at the
level of the operating system reasons about

00:20:23.760 --> 00:20:29.900
things rather than the level that we would
like to think about as experimentalists and

00:20:29.900 --> 00:20:31.290
so forth.

00:20:31.290 --> 00:20:36.170
So what we'd like to do is lift from these
chronological steps in a nod to something

00:20:36.170 --> 00:20:43.540
that says this thing was produced because
somebody in particular ran it using the following

00:20:43.540 --> 00:20:44.540
steps, and so on.

00:20:44.540 --> 00:20:51.070
It's really about causal relationships, not
individual ontological steps.

00:20:51.070 --> 00:20:56.370
And the kinds of questions we want to ask
are generally about relationships between

00:20:56.370 --> 00:21:01.880
the individual components that were processed,
created, et cetera.

00:21:01.880 --> 00:21:05.980
So this is going to motivate us to rethink
our level of abstraction.

00:21:05.980 --> 00:21:10.670
If this is too low level, can we come up with
something higher level?

00:21:10.670 --> 00:21:15.090
Second big problem is that in general, these
logs get really, really irregular.

00:21:15.090 --> 00:21:20.750
It's hard to process logs from different software
artifacts, because they just end up looking

00:21:20.750 --> 00:21:22.150
completely different.

00:21:22.150 --> 00:21:29.240
So a second goal as we think about provenance
is we need a standard way of capturing provenance

00:21:29.240 --> 00:21:33.950
no matter where it came from, what systems,
et cetera.

00:21:33.950 --> 00:21:41.380
So summary of this first part-- provenance,
we can break it into two main components.

00:21:41.380 --> 00:21:46.510
The first is taking raw data, capturing the
who, what, where, why, and how.

00:21:46.510 --> 00:21:50.600
This is primarily a data design, and then
ultimately a human plus

00:21:50.600 --> 00:21:53.720
automated data entry task.

00:21:53.720 --> 00:21:57.610
This step is, of course, extraordinarily important.

00:21:57.610 --> 00:22:03.270
But in many data science scenarios, what we
will end up sharing and reusing may not be

00:22:03.270 --> 00:22:04.270
the raw data.

00:22:04.270 --> 00:22:06.220
It might be some kind of derived data.

00:22:06.220 --> 00:22:13.580
So our focus, and especially the focus of
computer scientists like me, is on computational

00:22:13.580 --> 00:22:16.270
support for provenance on derived data.

00:22:16.270 --> 00:22:21.221
And this is really about capturing the individual
steps, the inputs, the conditions, and so

00:22:21.221 --> 00:22:28.730
on for each of the processing stages and really
describing not just fields, but relationships

00:22:28.730 --> 00:22:30.780
among the individual data products.

00:22:30.780 --> 00:22:38.120
Ideally, we'd do this completely automatically,
transparently, invisible to the scientist.

00:22:38.120 --> 00:22:43.700
In the worst case, we will do it semi-automatically
with the scientist potentially helping out

00:22:43.700 --> 00:22:47.490
a little bit with providing extra details.

00:22:47.490 --> 00:22:53.430
Other big piece is we want to make sure that
any computational support here has standards

00:22:53.430 --> 00:23:00.590
and a way that provenance is captured generically,
but then it's also extensible so that specific

00:23:00.590 --> 00:23:05.050
fields, specific metadata can be added for
a given domain.

00:23:05.050 --> 00:23:13.990
Finally, it's worth noting our goals, again,
are reproducibility, assessment of quality,

00:23:13.990 --> 00:23:16.269
harmonization, and so forth.

00:23:16.269 --> 00:23:27.309
So let's now take the goals from this first
segment and look at how we might refine the

00:23:27.309 --> 00:23:33.390
straw man approach into something a little
more suitable for reasoning about.

00:23:33.390 --> 00:23:39.860
And coming from the database community, we
tend to think a lot about data models, like

00:23:39.860 --> 00:23:42.890
the relational data model or a graph data
model.

00:23:42.890 --> 00:23:48.809
Similarly, what we want here is a provenance
data model or a provenance model, and that's

00:23:48.809 --> 00:23:52.960
basically what we're going to be talking about
next.

00:23:52.960 --> 00:23:58.030
So broadly, if we look at what we're trying
to capture in data provenance, there are really

00:23:58.030 --> 00:24:01.160
three main components.

00:24:01.160 --> 00:24:04.650
There are entities, which are the things that
are being manipulated.

00:24:04.650 --> 00:24:11.290
So typically, that could be something like
a data file or a data object within a file

00:24:11.290 --> 00:24:16.030
or a data object within a data stream from
a sensor.

00:24:16.030 --> 00:24:20.620
Somehow or other, you have these things being
fed into processing steps.

00:24:20.620 --> 00:24:25.670
And as a result, other entities are being
produced.

00:24:25.670 --> 00:24:29.260
So the first component is entities.

00:24:29.260 --> 00:24:41.860
The second thing is some of the entities--
for instance, pieces of code-- might be run

00:24:41.860 --> 00:24:46.470
as tasks and might be used to process data.

00:24:46.470 --> 00:24:49.210
So we have this generic idea of activities.

00:24:49.210 --> 00:24:57.110
Some of the activities might be processed
by humans, so conducting some particular manual

00:24:57.110 --> 00:25:02.670
step in processing is definitely an activity,
and we might want to abstract that.

00:25:02.670 --> 00:25:07.540
And obviously, a lot of the activities are
going to be running software artifacts, which

00:25:07.540 --> 00:25:13.451
really are, at some level, binary files with
particular versions and timestamps and so

00:25:13.451 --> 00:25:14.451
on.

00:25:14.451 --> 00:25:22.400
We're going to be running those and processing
input entities or files using particular parameters.

00:25:22.400 --> 00:25:33.240
So activities really represent not a piece
of code or a particular kind of noun in the

00:25:33.240 --> 00:25:39.550
system, but rather, the act of doing some
task, either computationally or manually.

00:25:39.550 --> 00:25:43.050
And then finally, we have agents, who typically
are people.

00:25:43.050 --> 00:25:48.320
The activities are going to be invoked by
the people.

00:25:48.320 --> 00:25:56.679
But sometimes, also, you will have situations
where a person starts one particular computational

00:25:56.679 --> 00:25:59.950
job and then spawns a bunch of others.

00:25:59.950 --> 00:26:06.840
So agents can be both humans, but they can
also be other tasks that then go on and invoke

00:26:06.840 --> 00:26:08.860
still other tasks and so on.

00:26:08.860 --> 00:26:17.780
So agents basically start activities, or put
the other way, activities are usually conducted

00:26:17.780 --> 00:26:21.390
on behalf of or by agents.

00:26:21.390 --> 00:26:30.800
Activities typically read entities and write
entities, or more generically, they have entities

00:26:30.800 --> 00:26:34.140
that they process in order to produce other
entities.

00:26:34.140 --> 00:26:42.510
So on the bottom of this slide, we have a
figure which shows, again, our gene sequencing

00:26:42.510 --> 00:26:47.860
example where we see sequence data which is
an entity.

00:26:47.860 --> 00:26:50.390
It's annotated by metadata.

00:26:50.390 --> 00:26:52.000
Sorry.

00:26:52.000 --> 00:26:59.480
And at the next step, we have this ellipse
represents the quality check activity, which

00:26:59.480 --> 00:27:00.480
has some parameters.

00:27:00.480 --> 00:27:07.210
And in fact, not shown here, the quality check
presumably also links to the particular file

00:27:07.210 --> 00:27:11.010
that is the program being run.

00:27:11.010 --> 00:27:15.680
The quality check is being performed by our
user.

00:27:15.680 --> 00:27:20.840
So here we have the little house symbol with
Bill Nye the Science Guy in it.

00:27:20.840 --> 00:27:23.730
Perhaps he ran the quality check code.

00:27:23.730 --> 00:27:30.580
The output of that will be these filtered
sequences, which again, is an entity.

00:27:30.580 --> 00:27:35.080
Then we run an aligner, which takes the filtered
sequences in a reference genome as input entities,

00:27:35.080 --> 00:27:37.330
and it produces alignments.

00:27:37.330 --> 00:27:41.310
That's essentially an abstract representation
of provenance.

00:27:41.310 --> 00:27:45.880
And in fact, I'm broadly following the notation
of one of the standards that we're going to

00:27:45.880 --> 00:27:46.880
talk about in a moment.

00:27:46.880 --> 00:27:52.070
One thing that's interesting about the standard
is that the arrows point in the opposite direction

00:27:52.070 --> 00:27:53.070
of data flow.

00:27:53.070 --> 00:27:58.730
So if you read this diagram initially, you
may get the impression that data is going

00:27:58.730 --> 00:27:59.760
from right to left.

00:27:59.760 --> 00:28:07.690
But in fact, in general, these edges, the
lines, and the arrows represent where things

00:28:07.690 --> 00:28:11.090
came from as opposed to where data was sent.

00:28:11.090 --> 00:28:17.050
So we're really looking at the alignments
were created by the aligner, which read as

00:28:17.050 --> 00:28:19.900
an input the filtered sequences.

00:28:19.900 --> 00:28:27.610
More generically, this standard, which at
this point has had a lot of input from many

00:28:27.610 --> 00:28:33.240
different constituencies and has become the
standard for data provenance, called PROV-DM,

00:28:33.240 --> 00:28:38.461
the Provenance Data Model, is standardized
by the World Wide Web Consortium, and it's

00:28:38.461 --> 00:28:42.270
actually one of a family of provenance standards.

00:28:42.270 --> 00:28:51.840
It basically defines a syntax, an abstract
representation, and a basic vocabulary for

00:28:51.840 --> 00:28:54.790
core aspects of data provenance.

00:28:54.790 --> 00:29:02.450
And so you'll see in the table on the left
this notion of entities, activities, and agents

00:29:02.450 --> 00:29:04.930
that I had used in the previous example.

00:29:04.930 --> 00:29:11.580
Underneath that, there is a series of relationships
between these entities, activities, and agents,

00:29:11.580 --> 00:29:21.830
so things like something was generated by
a particular activity, or that an agent used

00:29:21.830 --> 00:29:30.250
a particular entity, or that one particular
entity was derived from another, et cetera.

00:29:30.250 --> 00:29:37.929
So this is a basic core vocabulary for relationships
between data items.

00:29:37.929 --> 00:29:43.040
On the right, we see some examples of different
views of provenance graphs describing these

00:29:43.040 --> 00:29:44.040
relationships.

00:29:44.040 --> 00:29:51.730
It's worth noting that all of the white boxes
in those figures describe extra properties.

00:29:51.730 --> 00:29:58.330
This is metadata that's specific
to the domain that the data was acquired from.

00:29:58.330 --> 00:30:03.550
So these are the metadata fields that you
would customize for your particular experiment

00:30:03.550 --> 00:30:08.730
or data modality or data types.

00:30:08.730 --> 00:30:16.220
PROV tries to give a basic core for describing
entities, activities, agents, and relationships

00:30:16.220 --> 00:30:22.950
and assumes that you will augment all of that
with additional domain-specific fields that

00:30:22.950 --> 00:30:29.190
hopefully will be propagated in a straightforward
way from inputs to outputs and so on.

00:30:29.190 --> 00:30:36.049
So that's the basic core way of capturing
provenance in a general way that is also extensible.

00:30:36.049 --> 00:30:41.970
And in many ways, I like to make the analogy--
even though it's not an exact one-- that one

00:30:41.970 --> 00:30:49.870
should think of PROVDM much the way one thinks
of standards like XML or JSON or RDF, which

00:30:49.870 --> 00:30:55.970
is to say there is a core syntax that helps
us share a lot of the basic concepts, but

00:30:55.970 --> 00:31:02.090
ultimately, there will be extra fields, metadata,
and relationships that you will need to define

00:31:02.090 --> 00:31:06.410
and add as you go.

00:31:06.410 --> 00:31:14.110
Just to give some hint on how PROV-DM is actually
entered into the computer, there are several

00:31:14.110 --> 00:31:15.419
different formats.

00:31:15.419 --> 00:31:20.180
One of them is called PROV-N. It's designed
to be fairly human readable.

00:31:20.180 --> 00:31:25.230
So what we see in the example here is the
definition of a bunch of entities.

00:31:25.230 --> 00:31:34.510
So the first entity is some kind of article,
and we're assigning particular metadata fields,

00:31:34.510 --> 00:31:37.950
like a title, to that particular entity.

00:31:37.950 --> 00:31:43.600
Those things that you see before the colons--
the EXN and the DC terms and so on-- are part

00:31:43.600 --> 00:31:45.740
of what's called a namespace.

00:31:45.740 --> 00:31:52.059
It's a place where individual terms are being
defined, for instance, within an ontology

00:31:52.059 --> 00:31:54.690
or within a particular schema.

00:31:54.690 --> 00:32:00.040
So for the most part, you can ignore that
prefix and just read this as we're defining

00:32:00.040 --> 00:32:05.090
an entity which is an article, and it has
a title being crime rises in cities.

00:32:05.090 --> 00:32:10.220
We have similar definitions here for dataset
1 and region list and so on.

00:32:10.220 --> 00:32:18.040
There is also an XML equivalent format for
those who are building tools.

00:32:18.040 --> 00:32:23.080
As you can hopefully get from the example,
it's much less human readable.

00:32:23.080 --> 00:32:27.410
But the advantage to XML is that there are
many, many tools that automatically generate,

00:32:27.410 --> 00:32:31.440
parse, and read it.

00:32:31.440 --> 00:32:35.549
So that's what provenance looks like, ultimately.

00:32:35.549 --> 00:32:41.330
There's this abstract model of graphs between
users, agents, and activities, and then there's

00:32:41.330 --> 00:32:47.250
this more syntactic model, which is just how
we encode the basic objects and relationships

00:32:47.250 --> 00:32:48.280
within the graph.

00:32:48.280 --> 00:32:53.100
The next question, obviously, should be we
have this model, how in the world are we actually

00:32:53.100 --> 00:32:58.770
going to record, in particular, the provenance
relationships.

00:32:58.770 --> 00:33:00.980
So there are really three main ways.

00:33:00.980 --> 00:33:07.880
If you write a particular analytics algorithm,
then one thing that you could do-although

00:33:07.880 --> 00:33:13.550
it takes a fair amount of work-- would be
to have the program itself not only produce

00:33:13.550 --> 00:33:19.970
the output data files, but in fact, record
somewhere, for instance, a bunch of PROV-N

00:33:19.970 --> 00:33:25.830
or PROV-XML records describing exactly what
it did.

00:33:25.830 --> 00:33:31.280
But that's fairly labor intensive, and especially
for code that already exists, most people

00:33:31.280 --> 00:33:36.230
will not be particularly motivated to add
this extra instrumentation.

00:33:36.230 --> 00:33:40.929
So a second thing you can do-- and this has
been the common practice within a lot of the

00:33:40.929 --> 00:33:47.140
scientific communities, especially in areas
like physics and astronomy where there are

00:33:47.140 --> 00:33:55.360
very regular processing workflows done-- there
are special platforms, like Taverna and Kepler

00:33:55.360 --> 00:34:02.900
and Galaxy and VisTrails-- which basically
allow you to define sequences of computation

00:34:02.900 --> 00:34:08.240
using a boxes and arrows dataflow module.

00:34:08.240 --> 00:34:17.500
Actually, the [INAUDIBLE] effort also does
something similar to this, where different

00:34:17.500 --> 00:34:22.799
computational steps can be cascaded together,
usually using visual tools, and these will

00:34:22.799 --> 00:34:25.159
be automatically run on a server somewhere.

00:34:25.159 --> 00:34:32.869
And many of the existing workflow engines,
as a byproduct, will automatically generate

00:34:32.869 --> 00:34:35.929
data provenance to go along with the data
outputs.

00:34:35.929 --> 00:34:40.710
And this is a wonderful and extremely helpful
capability.

00:34:40.710 --> 00:34:46.989
However, there are a number of people-- and
I would count among that many of my collaborators--

00:34:46.989 --> 00:34:54.879
who feel that a large part of their data analysis
is not regular enough that they want to use

00:34:54.879 --> 00:34:56.349
a workflow system.

00:34:56.349 --> 00:35:04.830
Rather, they want to be able to go, for instance,
to the Python Shell and run a bunch of particular

00:35:04.830 --> 00:35:05.830
steps.

00:35:05.830 --> 00:35:13.910
Or they might want to go to the Linux terminal
and run different programs to do some sequence

00:35:13.910 --> 00:35:18.029
of steps, and they might want to be able to
change this on the fly.

00:35:18.029 --> 00:35:24.180
So for these kinds of scenarios, to this point,
there have not been very many answers for

00:35:24.180 --> 00:35:27.809
how to actually capture that kind of provenance.

00:35:27.809 --> 00:35:33.289
In recent years, there have been a number
of efforts to try to actually build tools

00:35:33.289 --> 00:35:39.059
that help capture arbitrary executions of
programs and scripts and so on.

00:35:39.059 --> 00:35:47.859
So at NYU Poly, Juliana Freire's group has
done a bunch of work in this space.

00:35:47.859 --> 00:35:54.309
And as I had mentioned earlier, our group
here has also done something in this space

00:35:54.309 --> 00:35:59.869
to allow for a background, Dropbox-like tool
that just watches what you're running, and

00:35:59.869 --> 00:36:02.759
records it, and generates PROV-DM.

00:36:02.759 --> 00:36:06.520
So I'll talk more about this, again, in a
little bit.

00:36:06.520 --> 00:36:11.410
So that was what provenance looks like, how
we actually record it.

00:36:11.410 --> 00:36:19.279
The last piece to provenance capture is where
do we actually put it once we have it there.

00:36:19.279 --> 00:36:24.789
Again, I want to emphasize that one shouldn't
really think of provenance and data as living

00:36:24.789 --> 00:36:27.069
in completely different worlds.

00:36:27.069 --> 00:36:32.979
However, we typically have a solution for
where we're storing our data, and it may not

00:36:32.979 --> 00:36:37.210
be easy to gracefully extend that to support
provenance.

00:36:37.210 --> 00:36:43.430
So in general, the way we think of provenance
storage is somewhere, we ought to have a provenance

00:36:43.430 --> 00:36:48.549
database which will really be strong graphs,
and there ought to be a way of having common

00:36:48.549 --> 00:36:56.930
identifiers between nodes in our traditional
database or data storage system and references

00:36:56.930 --> 00:37:02.559
in the provenance storage system so that we
can reconstruct the links between provenance

00:37:02.559 --> 00:37:03.579
and data.

00:37:03.579 --> 00:37:11.009
Often, when people hear that provenance and
PROV-DM are a graph, we'll pretty quickly

00:37:11.009 --> 00:37:16.450
gravitate toward graph databases, so things
like Neo4j and OrientDB and so on.

00:37:16.450 --> 00:37:20.049
And that's a perfectly good and viable place
to put provenance.

00:37:20.049 --> 00:37:27.170
But in fact, one can also encode provenance
in essentially any kind of NoSQL database,

00:37:27.170 --> 00:37:31.270
or relational databases, where the tables
would be things like entities and attributes

00:37:31.270 --> 00:37:33.910
and agents, et cetera.

00:37:33.910 --> 00:37:38.420
So prov does not
particularly prescribe what the underlying

00:37:38.420 --> 00:37:39.999
database is.

00:37:39.999 --> 00:37:46.349
However, it does pretty well prescribe what
we want to store within the database.

00:37:46.349 --> 00:37:54.059
And here, there are a lot of tools that have
been built from the PROV-DM spec.

00:37:54.059 --> 00:38:02.790
In particular, Luc Moreau, who was one of
the core leaders of the prov movement, has

00:38:02.790 --> 00:38:07.569
a very nice prov toolbox in open source.

00:38:07.569 --> 00:38:14.440
And a variety of groups, including ours, have
built expanded services over that for trying

00:38:14.440 --> 00:38:16.989
to capture provenance from the community.

00:38:16.989 --> 00:38:24.470
So we, for instance, have a server called
pennprovenance.net where you can just upload,

00:38:24.470 --> 00:38:32.220
share, and manage PROV-DM data.

00:38:32.220 --> 00:38:40.950
What I described so far is looking at provenance
in terms of files and computational modules,

00:38:40.950 --> 00:38:42.609
so things like programs.

00:38:42.609 --> 00:38:47.880
In reality, that is one piece of the broad
spectrum of how computer scientists think

00:38:47.880 --> 00:38:50.970
about provenance.

00:38:50.970 --> 00:38:57.210
There are a lot of efforts in trying to model
provenance at a finer level of granularity

00:38:57.210 --> 00:38:59.460
than files and programs.

00:38:59.460 --> 00:39:08.009
So for instance, I might look at my file that
had lists of gene sequences and want to specifically

00:39:08.009 --> 00:39:14.099
describe each of those sequences as a separate
input entity.

00:39:14.099 --> 00:39:20.180
And I might want to think about my aligner
program as having individual sub-steps.

00:39:20.180 --> 00:39:25.619
So we tend to think of this as going from
treating files and programs as black boxes

00:39:25.619 --> 00:39:31.550
that we don't understand into making them
clear boxes or gray boxes or white boxes where

00:39:31.550 --> 00:39:36.920
you can actually algorithmically reason about
what's going on in terms of the individual

00:39:36.920 --> 00:39:41.420
data objects, the contents, and in terms of
the individual operations.

00:39:41.420 --> 00:39:45.630
And if you go to this level of detail, which
has been studied heavily in the database community

00:39:45.630 --> 00:39:54.720
and others, this lets you reason in much more
detail about how specific results as opposed

00:39:54.720 --> 00:39:58.520
to specific files, how those were produced.

00:39:58.520 --> 00:40:04.210
And it can also, in many cases, help diagnose
why some results might be different from others,

00:40:04.210 --> 00:40:06.329
and so forth.

00:40:06.329 --> 00:40:12.869
So broadly, I think it's important to understand
that from the life sciences perspective, we'll

00:40:12.869 --> 00:40:20.869
often think about file to program to file
provenance, black box provenance.

00:40:20.869 --> 00:40:27.280
But there are situations where we need this
lower level of detail or finer level of detail,

00:40:27.280 --> 00:40:29.310
and there are technologies for that as well.

00:40:29.310 --> 00:40:37.420
That's not going to be the focus of my presentation
here, but there are many references in that.

00:40:37.420 --> 00:40:44.079
And in the Q&amp;A or in other scenarios, I'm
happy to talk about this in more detail.

00:40:44.079 --> 00:40:49.680
So to summarize part 2, we've gone from this
notion of why we want to capture provenance

00:40:49.680 --> 00:40:59.809
to the basic core constructs of how people
encode provenance abstractly as entities,

00:40:59.809 --> 00:41:02.779
activities, and agents.

00:41:02.779 --> 00:41:08.809
We talked a bit about the PROV-DM standard
and the fact that it captures data flows and

00:41:08.809 --> 00:41:12.790
relationships, but allows additional metadata
to be attached.

00:41:12.790 --> 00:41:18.440
We talked a bit about different tools and
mechanisms for capturing provenance, and then

00:41:18.440 --> 00:41:25.119
we highlighted the notion that actually, there
are also other, more detailed forms of the

00:41:25.119 --> 00:41:26.960
data provenance.

00:41:26.960 --> 00:41:33.239
As the last major segment, what I want to
do is talk briefly about some of the computational

00:41:33.239 --> 00:41:38.349
tasks that people are
doing or attempting to develop techniques

00:41:38.349 --> 00:41:41.289
to do with data provenance.

00:41:41.289 --> 00:41:48.140
So one of the most common things, and one
of the most commonly supported things that

00:41:48.140 --> 00:41:55.960
we can do, is to take data items-- so again,
files or individual records-- and to build

00:41:55.960 --> 00:42:05.230
tools that will help look up the provenance
of those particular objects and visualize

00:42:05.230 --> 00:42:15.200
it so that users can sanity check, can reproduce,
can identify why and how a particular data

00:42:15.200 --> 00:42:17.210
item existed.

00:42:17.210 --> 00:42:26.420
So this broadly is useful for sanity checking,
interpretation, and so on at the manual level.

00:42:26.420 --> 00:42:31.160
And it's not an uncommon operation at all,
especially if you want to reproduce results.

00:42:31.160 --> 00:42:35.730
But in many ways, this is the simplest thing,
because what it really involves is looking

00:42:35.730 --> 00:42:41.670
up the provenance, the PROV-DM entry for a
given entity, pulling up that piece of the

00:42:41.670 --> 00:42:46.640
graph, and then running it through interactive
visualization tools, like the one that I'm

00:42:46.640 --> 00:42:56.819
showing here, and a wide variety of tools
that are available to the community.

00:42:56.819 --> 00:43:01.890
While we're talking about this, I should also
mention, a question may come up if you're

00:43:01.890 --> 00:43:07.770
trying to do reproducible research, and especially
if you're trying to authenticate a result--

00:43:07.770 --> 00:43:13.210
how do we know that what we're visualizing
is actually what was done?

00:43:13.210 --> 00:43:18.210
What if somebody came into our provenance
database and hacked the provenance entries?

00:43:18.210 --> 00:43:25.759
Well, it turns out there are a bunch of pieces
of work in the computer science space-- in

00:43:25.759 --> 00:43:31.799
fact, done by my colleagues here at Penn--
specifically developing ways of using encryption

00:43:31.799 --> 00:43:39.839
and so-called Merkle trees to help capture
the individual steps and essentially authenticate

00:43:39.839 --> 00:43:46.869
that the provenance is intact and has not
been forged or modified in any given way.

00:43:46.869 --> 00:43:49.940
So that's provenance and its visualization.

00:43:49.940 --> 00:43:55.190
Some other things that we might want to do
are what I want to focus on for the last couple

00:43:55.190 --> 00:43:56.190
of minutes.

00:43:56.190 --> 00:44:01.329
I want to emphasize here that most of the
things I'm going to briefly talk about are

00:44:01.329 --> 00:44:03.400
on the active research side of the world.

00:44:03.400 --> 00:44:06.349
There are some preliminary tools for many
of them.

00:44:06.349 --> 00:44:13.000
But many of them are highly dependent on exactly
what level of detail we have in terms of the

00:44:13.000 --> 00:44:16.980
black box operations and how they work.

00:44:16.980 --> 00:44:23.079
And because they're so dramatically affected
by the individual assumptions of the operations,

00:44:23.079 --> 00:44:28.849
the number of tools available that are general
purpose is a bit more limited here.

00:44:28.849 --> 00:44:33.769
So these are all areas where I think we're
going to start to see more and more tooling

00:44:33.769 --> 00:44:39.739
and more and more options as the next couple
of years go forward.

00:44:39.739 --> 00:44:49.239
So first piece I'm going to mention is we
might go from visualizing provenance to reproduction

00:44:49.239 --> 00:44:54.809
to actually using provenance and automating
the process of reproducing an experiment.

00:44:54.809 --> 00:45:00.650
So basically, this involves, for instance,
having a tool where the user can identify

00:45:00.650 --> 00:45:07.819
some particular data product and say can you
repeat the same process that was involved

00:45:07.819 --> 00:45:13.529
in creating this data product on my new input
data item over here.

00:45:13.529 --> 00:45:20.049
So this is now really taking the provenance
and using it, essentially, to define a script

00:45:20.049 --> 00:45:22.839
to run against another new piece of data.

00:45:22.839 --> 00:45:29.839
And then we can further expand on that to
say why don't we reproduce and rerun exactly

00:45:29.839 --> 00:45:33.739
the script that was used
on the previous result with one small modification.

00:45:33.739 --> 00:45:39.160
So for instance, I want to replace one of
the original modules with my own module, and

00:45:39.160 --> 00:45:41.140
now I want to compare the results.

00:45:41.140 --> 00:45:46.470
So this task of automating the reproduction
and ultimately comparison and benchmarking

00:45:46.470 --> 00:45:54.099
of different results is something that's computationally
fairly easy to do, but can be incredibly effective

00:45:54.099 --> 00:46:03.359
as part of the exploration and reproduction
repertoire for scientists.

00:46:03.359 --> 00:46:09.099
Another big piece, and one of my personal
interests, is this interplay between data

00:46:09.099 --> 00:46:14.420
provenance and both trustworthiness of the
data and impact.

00:46:14.420 --> 00:46:19.640
So I had alluded a bit to this earlier.

00:46:19.640 --> 00:46:26.849
If we look at all of our data in some PROV-DM
graph, ultimately, what will happen is certain

00:46:26.849 --> 00:46:32.309
entities will be re-used in many, many, many
experiments, and perhaps the results of those

00:46:32.309 --> 00:46:37.539
experiments can be reused in many, many other
analyses, and so on.

00:46:37.539 --> 00:46:41.910
And if we have a bird's eye perspective of
what's being reused, this gives us a wonderful

00:46:41.910 --> 00:46:52.710
opportunity to assign scores to impact and
centrality and visibility of particular data

00:46:52.710 --> 00:46:53.710
items.

00:46:53.710 --> 00:47:01.529
And in the same way that we tend to use things
like the h-index for measuring scholarly impact

00:47:01.529 --> 00:47:08.599
or page rank for measuring authoritativeness
of websites, we can create a sharing index

00:47:08.599 --> 00:47:13.460
or an impact index on data based on who is
using it.

00:47:13.460 --> 00:47:18.380
And the building block to that is data provenance.

00:47:18.380 --> 00:47:25.349
Another big piece that's closely related but
now focuses on single items as opposed to

00:47:25.349 --> 00:47:34.430
the entire network of relationships is over
time, as we look at data and assess whether

00:47:34.430 --> 00:47:41.849
it is correct or incorrect, we can use machine
learning and other techniques to figure out

00:47:41.849 --> 00:47:46.140
between all the data items that are correct
and all the data items that are incorrect,

00:47:46.140 --> 00:47:50.249
or between all the data items that are relevant
and those that are irrelevant, what is it

00:47:50.249 --> 00:47:55.369
in their provenance that differentiates between
the good and the bad or the trusted and untrusted.

00:47:55.369 --> 00:48:01.150
And over time, we can actually learn to put
weights and scores and probabilities of relevance

00:48:01.150 --> 00:48:05.660
on data based purely on its provenance.

00:48:05.660 --> 00:48:10.930
So that's something that I think will continue
to become more and more important over time

00:48:10.930 --> 00:48:18.569
as more data becomes shared and reused in
more and more and more experiments.

00:48:18.569 --> 00:48:25.839
Really, the last main piece I wanted to briefly
allude to is provenance is also a building

00:48:25.839 --> 00:48:28.910
block toward diagnosis and debugging.

00:48:28.910 --> 00:48:35.440
I have collaborators who run, essentially,
scripts over their data on a daily basis.

00:48:35.440 --> 00:48:37.180
They are acquiring new data every day.

00:48:37.180 --> 00:48:38.609
They run a script.

00:48:38.609 --> 00:48:40.709
They produce some results.

00:48:40.709 --> 00:48:43.180
Over time, they make tweaks to the script.

00:48:43.180 --> 00:48:47.619
Over time, they might want to do a historical
comparison of their results from today versus

00:48:47.619 --> 00:48:49.650
the results from six months.

00:48:49.650 --> 00:48:53.989
And sometimes, the results aren't consistent.

00:48:53.989 --> 00:48:57.910
Now the question is how do they diagnose what
changed and why.

00:48:57.910 --> 00:49:02.910
And there, absolutely data provenance becomes
critical to really identifying the differences

00:49:02.910 --> 00:49:07.490
between today's script and six months ago's
script.

00:49:07.490 --> 00:49:13.099
And in fact, we can replay pieces of those
two scripts and compare them in order to ultimately

00:49:13.099 --> 00:49:18.539
help the scientist to debug what changed and
potentially what went wrong in their analysis.

00:49:18.539 --> 00:49:23.589
So this is something that we're very, very
interested in and actively working on here

00:49:23.589 --> 00:49:26.400
at Penn.

00:49:26.400 --> 00:49:33.940
One last part related to provenance that's
worth emphasizing before we conclude is that

00:49:33.940 --> 00:49:37.930
while I talked about going from this very
low level view of provenance to something

00:49:37.930 --> 00:49:42.521
more abstract, in fact, there are many possible
levels of abstraction I might want to have

00:49:42.521 --> 00:49:43.521
for provenance.

00:49:43.521 --> 00:49:45.859
In some cases, I want to see each individual
step.

00:49:45.859 --> 00:49:50.099
In some places, I may want to actually simplify
or collapse together many provenance steps

00:49:50.099 --> 00:49:53.119
into one unified black box.

00:49:53.119 --> 00:50:00.999
So one of the big tasks in this view of ultimately
building a provenance database or provenance

00:50:00.999 --> 00:50:06.079
support platform is we want to be able to
do queries and views that actually abstract

00:50:06.079 --> 00:50:07.079
data.

00:50:07.079 --> 00:50:12.959
And a lot of work done by my colleague Susan
Davidson has actually focused especially on

00:50:12.959 --> 00:50:15.569
those particular problems.

00:50:15.569 --> 00:50:18.400
So in terms of making provenance useful, we
can visualize it.

00:50:18.400 --> 00:50:20.980
We can use it to measure trust and impact.

00:50:20.980 --> 00:50:28.640
We can use it to measure differences between
different results, and we can use it to hide

00:50:28.640 --> 00:50:33.890
and abstract pieces of provenance and give
us a better perspective on what really happened.

00:50:33.890 --> 00:50:40.529
So let me wrap up now by saying I have tried
to capture, through the last 50 minutes, why

00:50:40.529 --> 00:50:44.809
provenance is actually a hard problem, and
it's more than just defining that there are

00:50:44.809 --> 00:50:46.950
data acquisition parameters.

00:50:46.950 --> 00:50:51.770
We described a bit about how we model provenance
abstractly, some of the tools and techniques

00:50:51.770 --> 00:50:56.680
available for capture, some of the tools and
techniques available are under active study

00:50:56.680 --> 00:50:58.849
for what we can do with provenance.

00:50:58.849 --> 00:51:04.859
I've mentioned along the way that as part
of our NIH BD2K project, we've been trying

00:51:04.859 --> 00:51:08.289
to build reusable tools for the community
in this space.

00:51:08.289 --> 00:51:11.069
So I'll leave a pointer up for that.

00:51:11.069 --> 00:51:17.440
And I want to conclude by saying provenance
is still a space that is in its early stages.

00:51:17.440 --> 00:51:21.390
There is tremendous opportunity for using
this kind of metadata for reasoning about

00:51:21.390 --> 00:51:22.440
data.

00:51:22.440 --> 00:51:28.680
And I'm very excited about where it's all
headed, and I expect in five years a repeat

00:51:28.680 --> 00:51:32.631
version of this webinar will end up looking
dramatically different as we start to have

00:51:32.631 --> 00:51:33.880
impact.

00:51:33.880 --> 00:51:41.950
So thank you for your time, and I'm happy
to flip to Q&amp;A mode as people have questions.

00:51:41.950 --> 00:51:42.950
Wonderful.

00:51:42.950 --> 00:51:43.950
Thank you so much, Zach.

00:51:43.950 --> 00:51:44.950
That was outstanding.

00:51:44.950 --> 00:51:51.219
So everyone, if you do have some questions
for Dr. Ives, please enter them into the little

00:51:51.219 --> 00:51:56.849
question functionality there that's part of
the Go To Webinar interface, probably on the

00:51:56.849 --> 00:51:59.880
right side of your screen, and we'll try and
get to those.

00:51:59.880 --> 00:52:02.329
Now, we've already had a few of these.

00:52:02.329 --> 00:52:10.749
And Zach, one person asked how much activity
is going on with the level of manufacturers

00:52:10.749 --> 00:52:18.420
to make sure that critical provenance information,
such as the firmware or the make, model, and

00:52:18.420 --> 00:52:24.319
serial number of the scanner or the gene sequencer
or whatever, is getting embedded into raw

00:52:24.319 --> 00:52:29.589
data files so that-- it's almost like-- it
there a minimum information standard that's

00:52:29.589 --> 00:52:34.309
available, and are manufacturers subscribing
to it?

00:52:34.309 --> 00:52:41.839
So that's a wonderful question, and I would
say it's highly, highly variable by communities.

00:52:41.839 --> 00:52:49.069
So it seems like there
are certain domains where most of that information

00:52:49.069 --> 00:52:51.599
does seem to be getting captured.

00:52:51.599 --> 00:52:57.359
I think there are also plenty of cases where
it's clear that that's not all being captured.

00:52:57.359 --> 00:53:02.650
It feels like this is one of the things that,
at the end of the day, the research community

00:53:02.650 --> 00:53:09.970
needs to articulate strongly to the manufacturers
that part of what's going to help us select

00:53:09.970 --> 00:53:14.369
your instrument will be that it records all
this kind of information.

00:53:14.369 --> 00:53:19.390
I suspect there are a lot of cases where people
have firmware that you will never see that

00:53:19.390 --> 00:53:24.560
that particular firmware got changed or whatnot.

00:53:24.560 --> 00:53:28.049
Many of the other parameters, I think, are
being captured and are available, at least

00:53:28.049 --> 00:53:30.589
in the manufacturer's file formats.

00:53:30.589 --> 00:53:35.769
I think the other thing that's always a bit
tricky is depending on the field, sometimes

00:53:35.769 --> 00:53:42.819
there is a level of detail that is output
by the instruments in their proprietary file

00:53:42.819 --> 00:53:49.640
formats, but then once you export that data
into some more open format, a lot of that

00:53:49.640 --> 00:53:52.049
detail tends to get dropped.

00:53:52.049 --> 00:53:58.489
So I think there's also an opportunity to
think about for specific data modalities,

00:53:58.489 --> 00:54:03.690
are there things that we want to encourage
everybody to propagate from the individual

00:54:03.690 --> 00:54:04.760
devices.

00:54:04.760 --> 00:54:12.979
And you also mentioned that you don't want
provenance to be a burden for researchers.

00:54:12.979 --> 00:54:19.190
With the exception of the manufacturers helping
us to do it, do you see any other way to achieve

00:54:19.190 --> 00:54:25.940
it being a simple thing for people to do,
and are there any editors or things that people

00:54:25.940 --> 00:54:28.019
can use to help store their provenance?

00:54:28.019 --> 00:54:33.970
That's a great question, and it has many,
many different answers.

00:54:33.970 --> 00:54:43.289
So I will mention I remember that the Case
Western Data Provenance Project that's going

00:54:43.289 --> 00:54:46.890
on in BD2K right now, Satya Sahoo's group,
one of the things that they're actually working

00:54:46.890 --> 00:54:54.209
on is trying to have forms and capture of
provenance as doctors see patients.

00:54:54.209 --> 00:54:56.739
That ends up being something that has to be
manually entered.

00:54:56.739 --> 00:55:03.670
There has been a lot of work in, for instance,
digital lab notebooks that use tablets.

00:55:03.670 --> 00:55:09.749
And one of the opportunities there, although
it's still fledgling, is if you have an iPad

00:55:09.749 --> 00:55:18.170
and there are, for instance, barcodes or RFIDs
or Bluetooth transponders or various other

00:55:18.170 --> 00:55:27.119
devices on, for instance, things in the physical
world, you can imagine extending your digital

00:55:27.119 --> 00:55:31.049
lab notebook to record all of those individual
things.

00:55:31.049 --> 00:55:37.109
So as we move to this future internet of things
world where all of your instruments can talk,

00:55:37.109 --> 00:55:42.609
I suspect we're going to see more and more
automatic data acquisition for these kinds

00:55:42.609 --> 00:55:46.940
of sensors, and what was used, and so on.

00:55:46.940 --> 00:55:53.089
Again, things like tablets would probably
help also as we switch from physical lab notebooks.

00:55:53.089 --> 00:55:59.289
But unfortunately, there is also no magic
wand technology here that's going to completely

00:55:59.289 --> 00:56:08.339
eliminate the human as the conduit for a huge
part of that knowledge.

00:56:08.339 --> 00:56:15.819
That being said, I think as particular tasks
mature, there probably will be more and more

00:56:15.819 --> 00:56:18.700
automation of specific steps there.

00:56:18.700 --> 00:56:19.700
Right.

00:56:19.700 --> 00:56:27.219
What role do you see librarians and archivists,
basically part of the digital archiving community--

00:56:27.219 --> 00:56:30.289
what role
can they play in helping scientists and data

00:56:30.289 --> 00:56:34.150
scientists to do this in an effective way?

00:56:34.150 --> 00:56:42.670
I think that's one of the great opportunities
for the library and information science community,

00:56:42.670 --> 00:56:52.839
is really helping to define and assist with
best practices and metadata, potentially actually

00:56:52.839 --> 00:56:59.609
aiding with the maintenance of archives, and
so on.

00:56:59.609 --> 00:57:05.309
I will say from my own interactions with,
for instance, our university librarians versus

00:57:05.309 --> 00:57:13.809
the libraries in other universities, it's
still very much an open question of just how

00:57:13.809 --> 00:57:17.449
central libraries are going to be in this
picture.

00:57:17.449 --> 00:57:21.739
I personally think this seems like a great
role for them.

00:57:21.739 --> 00:57:26.589
And there are a lot of really talented people
in those fields who are working broadly in

00:57:26.589 --> 00:57:34.450
this space and broadly in digital curation,
and digital citation, and so on.

00:57:34.450 --> 00:57:39.719
So my hope is that that will be among their
central roles looking forward.

00:57:39.719 --> 00:57:45.430
But it also feels like that is happening for
some, but not all, institutions at this point

00:57:45.430 --> 00:57:46.549
in time.

00:57:46.549 --> 00:57:47.549
Great.

00:57:47.549 --> 00:57:51.000
One final question for you-- is there any
way-- and perhaps you discussed some of this,

00:57:51.000 --> 00:57:56.499
but maybe you want to elaborate-- is there
any way to fingerprint data and its metadata

00:57:56.499 --> 00:58:01.829
and its provenance in such a way that there's
a unique identifier?

00:58:01.829 --> 00:58:10.219
Perhaps it's some sort of a hash number that
can be a unique identifier that can also help

00:58:10.219 --> 00:58:14.339
you to make sure that data hasn't been tampered
with or altered in any way?

00:58:14.339 --> 00:58:15.469
Yeah.

00:58:15.469 --> 00:58:19.529
So in fact, absolutely that is the case.

00:58:19.529 --> 00:58:32.219
There are many cryptographic hash algorithms
that people commonly use over the data to

00:58:32.219 --> 00:58:33.900
generate unique IDs.

00:58:33.900 --> 00:58:39.459
Sometimes, that ID itself is what is shared.

00:58:39.459 --> 00:58:47.470
Sometimes, that ID becomes a signature or
certificate on the data.

00:58:47.470 --> 00:58:52.069
One of the challenges tends to be that at
times, those signatures themselves are fairly

00:58:52.069 --> 00:58:53.400
large.

00:58:53.400 --> 00:59:01.039
At times, there is a question of how you would
create a signature or to look up a data item

00:59:01.039 --> 00:59:04.410
if you don't know all of its fields.

00:59:04.410 --> 00:59:09.279
So I think this is an extremely important
building block toward that.

00:59:09.279 --> 00:59:15.529
I think also, coming back to the point of
security, I think one of the other pieces

00:59:15.529 --> 00:59:21.490
that we have to take into account is often,
I'll acquire some data, I'll do an experiment,

00:59:21.490 --> 00:59:23.019
I'll produce an output.

00:59:23.019 --> 00:59:28.690
I might hash or assign that output, then I
give it to somebody else, who in turn does

00:59:28.690 --> 00:59:30.359
the same thing, and so on.

00:59:30.359 --> 00:59:35.130
And so really, you're building a, if you will,
a Russian doll of nested one thing inside

00:59:35.130 --> 00:59:37.539
of another inside of another.

00:59:37.539 --> 00:59:43.410
And so cryptography helps us tremendously
with that.

00:59:43.410 --> 00:59:49.249
But one of the major challenges there becomes
how do you look up the cryptographic key of

00:59:49.249 --> 00:59:53.260
a particular data item that you know exists
if you don't have the key in the first place.

00:59:53.260 --> 00:59:58.730
So it's really important and useful, and a
critical building block, but one has to realize

00:59:58.730 --> 01:00:03.269
that also, there are some particular use cases
that it doesn't support.

01:00:03.269 --> 01:00:04.269
OK.

01:00:04.269 --> 01:00:05.269
Wonderful.

